{"name": "set_size_amplified_evidence", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "People infer the hidden rule as the current best concept learner does: primitive-built rules with simplicity costs, hundred-chart ranges, and a preference for \"prime\" over \"odd\". The one distortion is how they pool evidence across examples. They do not treat each example as an independent draw that adds a fixed amount of evidence. Instead, examples that agree with each other are more convincing together than one at a time, so the weight each example carries grows with how many examples are shown, at a fitted rate. As a result, people commit to a tight rule or range only weakly from a single example and generalise broadly from it, but commit more sharply than an ideal learner once three or four examples agree.", "context": "experiment2 round 3 candidate 2 lens 4"}
{"name": "hidden_triples_concepts", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `prime_over_odd_reading`. Everything is kept: primitive-built rules with a per-primitive cost, the final-digit discount, the costs on powers/cubes and squares, times-table-graded multiples, Erlang ranges with a bonus for whole hundred-chart rows, the single preference moving plausibility from \"odd numbers\" to \"prime numbers\", the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment. The one change is that \"multiples of 3\" is hard to notice as a rule and carries its own fitted extra prior cost. Multiples of 3 have no visible signature in the written numeral: they cycle through every final digit and alternate between odd and even, so membership has to be checked by a digit-sum test. As a result, people rarely read odd multiples of 3 (69, 9, 39, 21; 63, 27, 99, 33) or mixed sets (39, 60, 12, 45) as \"multiples of 3\". They extend such sets by parity, final digit or range instead, and say no to other multiples of 3 (45, 57, 3, 24) more often than the incumbent predicts.", "context": "experiment2 round 3 candidate 3 refine incumbent prime_over_odd_reading"}
{"name": "fives_readable_multiples", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of the incumbent `prime_over_odd_reading`, with everything in it kept: primitive-built rules with a per-primitive cost, the final-digit discount, costs on powers/cubes and squares, times-table-graded multiples, the single preference moving plausibility from \"odd\" to \"prime\", Erlang ranges with a bonus for whole hundred-chart rows, the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment. The one change is that \"multiples of 5\" is unlike the other times tables: membership can be read straight off the numeral (it ends in 0 or 5), and counting by fives is a drilled skill, so the rule gets its own fitted prior bonus. So after sets such as 90, 100, 5, people commit to \"multiples of 5\" and say yes to 15, 25, 35 ... 95 almost always and no to 16, 92 or 93, more sharply than the incumbent predicts, while sets that fit other times tables are unaffected.", "context": "experiment2 round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent prime_over_odd_reading"}
{"name": "hidden_divisibility_by_three", "outcome": "admitted", "detail": "", "hypothesis": "Refinement of `prime_salient_marked_odd` (everything kept: primitive-built rules with a per-primitive cost, final-digit discount, costs on powers/cubes and squares, times-table-graded multiples, Erlang ranges with a bonus for whole hundred-chart rows, a prime bonus, a marked-odd cost, the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment). The one change is that divisibility by 3 cannot be read off a numeral's last digit the way divisibility by 2, 5 or 10 can; it needs a digit-sum check. So every rule whose membership test contains that check (\"multiples of 3\", \"of 6\", \"of 9\", \"of 12\") carries one shared, fitted extra prior cost. As a result, after odd multiples of 3 such as 69, 9, 39, 21 or 63, 27, 99, 33, people seldom commit to \"multiples of 3\". They extend \"odd\", a final digit or a range instead, and say yes to odd non-multiples of 3 (19, 29, 49, 23, 37) about as readily as to other multiples of 3.", "context": "experiment2 round 3 candidate 5 refine chosen"}
{"name": "multiples_of_any_step_size", "outcome": "dropped", "detail": "fold 0 too slow to fit: the fit of 'multiples_of_any_step_size' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "People's \"multiples\" concepts are not confined to the school times tables (3 to 12): they can skip-count by any step size that lands on at least two numbers in 1-100 (counting by 14s, 25s or 33s), and such large-step rules are about as easy to entertain as other two-part rules, at one fitted extra cost. Because these rules are small, the size principle makes them win when the examples fit them. So after 3, 33, 99 people read \"counting by 33s\", say yes to 66 and no to 23. After 14, 98, 91 they read \"counting by 14s\" with one stray example, and say no to 7 and 77.", "context": "experiment2 round 3"}
{"name": "boundary_landmark_stray_dismissal", "outcome": "dropped", "detail": "fold 0 too slow to fit: the fit of 'boundary_landmark_stray_dismissal' (target_accept 0.99) was still sampling after the 30-minute limit and was stopped. Every sampling run of a candidate's admission fit has a 30-minute limit. Make the model cheaper to evaluate and easier to sample: vectorise the likelihood over trials (no Python loops, pytensor scan or per-trial subgraphs), compute features once per unique sequence (in compute_features or prepare_observed, not in the graph), and drop or merge parameters the data barely constrain — a weakly identified posterior makes NUTS take maximal-length trajectories.", "hypothesis": "People treat the boundary numbers 1 and 100 differently from other examples: when one of them is shown, they suspect it marks the edge of the number line the program works on rather than a sample of its rule, so they are more ready to set it aside as a stray when inferring the concept. Everything else in how they infer the rule is as in the best model (primitive-built rules with simplicity costs, final-digit discount, costs on powers and squares, times-table-graded multiples, hundred-chart row ranges, \"prime\" preferred over \"odd\", the size principle and near-all-or-none commitment). So in sets such as 31, 19, 49, 100 or 20, 8, 84, 100 or 31, 3, 1, people generalise from the remaining examples (to nearby numbers or their shared rule) instead of stretching a range or rule to cover 1 or 100.", "context": "experiment2 round 3"}
